    category: "重點關注",
    title: "歐盟依據 DMA 法案，強制 Google 在 Android 系統開放 AI 平台與搜尋數據，以促進市場競爭",
    summary: "歐盟委員會（European Commission）援引《數位市場法案》（DMA），針對科技巨頭 Google 宣布新的強制性措施，旨在提升歐盟市場的互通性與競爭。這些新規定涵蓋 Google 的 Android 系統和搜尋服務兩大業務。在 Android 方面，Google 將被迫開放給第三方 AI 平台，目前 Google 的 Gemini AI 具有系統預載和「Hey Google」熱詞的優先權。歐盟監管機構指出，用戶應能安裝任何選擇的 AI 系統，且不應失去功能。雖然 Google 聲稱此舉會損害隱私與安全，但作為「守門人」（gatekeeper），Google 必須遵守這些法律約束。這些措施的實務影響是，它將迫使 Google 降低其 AI 服務的排他性，讓更多元的 AI 選擇進入 Android 生態系，從而增加用戶的選擇權，但同時也引發了關於用戶隱私和數據被大量使用的擔憂。",
    tags: ["歐盟", "DMA", "Google", "Android", "AI 平台", "市場競爭"],
    title_en: "EU Forces Google to Open Android System and Search Data for AI Platforms under DMA to Boost Market Competition",
    summary_en: "The European Commission, citing the Digital Markets Act (DMA), has announced new mandatory measures targeting tech giant Google, aiming to enhance interoperability and competition within the EU market. These new regulations cover two of Google's major operations: the Android system and its search services. Regarding Android, Google will be forced to open up to third-party AI platforms, as Google's own Gemini AI currently holds system pre-installation and 'Hey Google' hotword priority. EU regulators point out that users must be able to install any chosen AI system without losing functionality. Although Google claims these measures will harm privacy and security, as a 'gatekeeper,' Google must comply with these legal constraints. The practical implication of these measures is that they will compel Google to reduce the exclusivity of its AI services, allowing more diverse AI options into the Android ecosystem, thereby increasing user choice, but simultaneously raising concerns about user privacy and the extensive use of data.",
    tags_en: ["EU", "DMA", "Google", "Android", "AI Platform", "Market Competition"],
    sources: [
      { name: "Ars Technica", url: "https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/07/its-official-eu-will-force-google-to-share-search-data-and-open-up-ai-on-android", lang: "EN" }
    ]
